  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Moderate to Major tidal flooding persists\nthrough Friday. \n\nEvening update...The large cutoff low over the southern\nAppalachians this evening will slowly continue pushing\nnorth/east tonight. The region remains socked in a strong CAD\nfrom the very strong surface high over New England. The main\nsurface low has now departed well to our north, so all of the\nrain and wind from earlier has passed through. A lingering heavy\nmist slowly wanes overnight as southwesterly flow increases.\n\nPrevious Discussion...\n\nLatest sfc observational trends and LWX VWP showed that sfc \nwinds and winds aloft have likely peaked across the area as low\npressure center nears the area. The strongest gust seen so far\ntoday was at DCA with a gust to 54 kt (62 mph) at 1020 AM. The \nlow pressure center is currently located near Ladysmith VA and \nwill move north into Pennsylvania by 06Z tonight. The bulk of \nthe rain will move north of the Washington metro area by 4 PM, \nbut linger longer across northeast MD until about 7-8PM. A lull \nin precip will occur this evening as mid-level dry slot moves \nin. Overnight, upslope showers will return to the mountains with\nrain turning into snow showers around daybreak Fri. \n\nHeading into Friday, the focus shifts to the Allegheny Front as\nan extended period of upslope aided rain/snow showers will \noccur. Deep-layered westerlies are not the most ideal with \nupslope, but net snow accumulations around an inch or two are in\nthe forecast. Otherwise, the big story for the day will be \nanother round of brisk winds across the entire area. Initial \nwinds start off out of the south on Friday morning before \nshifting over to west to southwesterly for the second half of \nthe day. The passage of the upper low should make for an \nexpansive stratocumulus deck which offset some of the daytime \nheating. After spending Thanksgiving Day in the 40s and 50s, \nexpect much of the same for Friday (30s along the Alleghenies). \nSouthwesterly winds will gust around 25 to 35 mph, while \nremaining elevated into Friday night over the higher terrain. \nWesterly gusts overnight could approach 40 to 50 mph along the \nAllegheny Front. \n\nThe powerful Thanksgiving Day system finally reaches the\nCanadian Maritimes by Saturday. This will provide increasing\nsunshine through the day, much less wind, along with a gradual \nwarm up. Saturday's forecast highs are in the 50s to near 60 \ndegrees, with upper 30s to 40s across mountain locations. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry conditions Sunday, followed by precipitation Sunday night \nand early Monday, then dry again Monday afternoon through Wednesday.\n\nHigh pressure will keep the region dry Sunday. Temperatures will be \nnear seasonable with highs middle 50s to lower 60s.\n\nAs the high pushes further to the southeast Sunday night, a low \npressure system will move from the middle Mississippi Valley on \nSunday toward the eastern Great Lakes Sunday night. A trailing cold \nfront will push across the region Sunday night into Monday morning.\nShowers will likely accompany the front. We can't rule out a clap of \nthunder or two either given that there will be plenty of wind shear, \ndespite instability being minimal to nil and the time of night for \nfrontal passage. Highs cooler Sunday with highs mainly in lower to \nupper 50s.\n\nOnce the front moves to the east and southeast Monday afternoon, \nhigh pressure will become reestablished and bring dry conditions \nand seasonable temperatures to the region Monday through \nWednesday.",
